      "text": "To find the sum of all \\( x \\) that satisfy the equation \\( f^{-1}(x) = f\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) \\), we need to proceed step by step.\n\n1. **Find the inverse function \\( f^{-1}(x) \\) of \\( f(x) \\):**\n\n   The function \\( f(x) \\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = 16x + 3\n   \\]\n\n   To find the inverse, solve for \\( x \\) in terms of \\( y \\) where \\( y = f(x) \\):\n   \\[\n   y = 16x + 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y - 3 = 16x\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{y - 3}{16}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, the inverse function is:\n   \\[\n   f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{x - 3}{16}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Substitute \\( f\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) \\) into the given equation \\( f^{-1}(x) = f\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) \\):**\n\n   The equation we need to solve is:\n   \\[\n   f^{-1}(x) = f\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right)\n   \\]\n   Substitute \\( f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{x - 3}{16} \\) and \\( f\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) = 16 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2x} + 3 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x - 3}{16} = 16 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2x} + 3\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify and solve the equation:**\n\n   Multiply every term by 16 to clear the fraction:\n   \\[\n   x - 3 = 16 \\cdot \\frac{16}{2x} + 48\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x - 3 = \\frac{256}{2x} + 48\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x - 3 = \\frac{128}{x} + 48\n   \\]\n\n   Isolate the terms involving \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   x - \\frac{128}{x} = 48 + 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x - \\frac{128}{x} = 51\n   \\]\n\n   Multiply both sides by \\( x \\) to clear the denominator:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 128 = 51x\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 51x - 128 = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Solve the quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -51 \\), and \\( c = -128 \\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{(-51)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-128)}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{2601 + 512}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{3113}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The solutions are:\n   \\[\n   x_1 = \\frac{51 + \\sqrt{3113}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_2 = \\frac{51 - \\sqrt{3113}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Find the sum of the solutions:**\n\n   The sum of the solutions of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by \\( -\\frac{b}{a} \\). For our equation \\( x^2 - 51x - 128 = 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{-51}{1} = 51\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all \\( x \\) that satisfy the equation \\( f^{-1}(x) = f\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{51}\n\\]",
