      "text": "1. First, find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$: Since $y=f(x)=16x+3$, let $x=16y+3$. Then, $y=\\dfrac{x-3}{16}$. Thus, $f^{-1}(x)=\\boxed{\\dfrac{x-3}{16}}$.\n2. Given that $f^{-1}(x)=f((2x)^{-1})$, let $y=f((2x)^{-1})$. Since $16(2x)+3=f((2x)^{-1})$, we have $y=f^{-1}(\\dfrac{1}{2x})=y=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{2x}-3}{16}=\\dfrac{1}{2x}-\\dfrac{3}{16}$. Solve for $x$: \n   - Multiply both sides by $32x$ to eliminate the fraction: $32y=16x-\\dfrac{3}{2}x$\n   - Simplify: $32y=\\dfrac{31}{2}x\\Rightarrow x=\\dfrac{65}{64y}$\n3. Substitute $x=\\dfrac{65}{64y}$ into the negative $x$ equation to find the values of $y$: \n   - We have: $y=\\dfrac{65}{64y}-\\dfrac{3}{16}$\n   - Rearrange: $\\dfrac{65}{64y}-\\dfrac{3}{16}=\\dfrac{65}{64y}-\\dfrac{12}{64y}-\\dfrac{2}{64y}=\\dfrac{65-12-2}{64y}$\n   - Simplify: $\\dfrac{65-12-2}{64y}=\\dfrac{V_y}{y^2}=\\dfrac{376}{64}(\\neq 0)\\uparrow\\Rightarrow x^2＞64$\n4. Notice that $x^2-64$ is a positive solution to the inequality $f((2x)^{-1})=f^{-1}(x)$, so $x＞8$ or $x＜-8$\n5. The sum of all such $x$ is given by $(8-(-8)) = \\boxed{16}$.\n(The end)",
